1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,985
Lesson 2.7.

2
00:00:02,365 --> 00:00:03,556
Numerical SDEs.

3
00:00:04,683 --> 00:00:08,855
Almost no stochastic differential equation
can be solved with pencil and paper.

4
00:00:09,235 --> 00:00:13,331
To simulate a double well, a
continuous Ising model or a

5
00:00:13,331 --> 00:00:17,354
thermodynamic linear solver
we need to discretize time.

6
00:00:17,734 --> 00:00:22,262
This lesson presents the most used
method, Euler–Maruyama, and its

7
00:00:22,262 --> 00:00:26,447
two risks: instability, if the
step is large, and bias, which

8
00:00:26,447 --> 00:00:30,906
contaminates the statistics even
when the simulation looks right.

9
00:00:32,017 --> 00:00:34,390
The idea is Euler's, plus noise.

10
00:00:34,770 --> 00:00:40,054
At each step, we advance with the force
evaluated at the current point for

11
00:00:40,054 --> 00:00:44,696
a time Δt, and add a Wiener
increment, a Gaussian of variance Δt.

12
00:00:45,076 --> 00:00:48,934
Notice the scales: the
deterministic term is of

13
00:00:48,934 --> 00:00:52,547
order Δt, but the noise
is of order root Δt.

14
00:00:52,927 --> 00:00:56,057
That is why, for small
steps, noise dominates each

15
00:00:56,057 --> 00:00:59,751
individual step, while the
force dominates over long times.

16
00:01:00,883 --> 00:01:06,878
Let's apply the method to ẋ = −λ
x, the deterministic part of an OU.

17
00:01:07,258 --> 00:01:10,855
Each step multiplies x by 1 − λ Δt.

18
00:01:11,235 --> 00:01:14,432
If that factor has absolute
value less than one,

19
00:01:14,432 --> 00:01:17,496
the simulation decays;
if larger, it blows up.

20
00:01:17,876 --> 00:01:20,906
The condition is Δt < 2/λ.

21
00:01:21,286 --> 00:01:28,047
With λ = 10, a step of 0.15 oscillates
but converges; one of 0.21 blows up.

22
00:01:28,427 --> 00:01:34,644
In several dimensions the largest
eigenvalue rules: Δt < 2/λ_max.

23
00:01:35,024 --> 00:01:38,232
And since slow relaxation
takes 1/λ_min, the

24
00:01:38,232 --> 00:01:41,586
number of steps grows
as the condition number.

25
00:01:42,717 --> 00:01:47,613
Even when stable, the method does not
sample exactly the right distribution.

26
00:01:47,993 --> 00:01:52,928
For the OU, the stationary
variance of the discrete chain is

27
00:01:52,928 --> 00:01:57,288
1/(β λ (1 − λ Δt/2)), a bit
larger than the true one.

28
00:01:57,668 --> 00:02:03,962
The curve shows the relative bias as a
function of the step: it is about λ Δt/2.

29
00:02:04,342 --> 00:02:08,810
With a step equal to half the
stability limit, the variance doubles.

30
00:02:09,190 --> 00:02:13,541
For the OU there is an exact
alternative: use the factor

31
00:02:13,541 --> 00:02:17,193
e^(−λ Δt) and noise with
the matching variance.

32
00:02:18,317 --> 00:02:21,456
How fast does the simulation
improve as the step shrinks?

33
00:02:21,836 --> 00:02:25,429
We measure the strong error,
the average distance between the

34
00:02:25,429 --> 00:02:28,904
simulated and the exact trajectory
with the same noise, for

35
00:02:28,904 --> 00:02:32,144
geometric Brownian motion,
which has an exact solution.

36
00:02:32,523 --> 00:02:37,017
On a log scale, Euler–Maruyama
drops with slope one half:

37
00:02:37,017 --> 00:02:41,118
you must divide the step
by four to halve the error.

38
00:02:41,498 --> 00:02:45,617
The Milstein method,
which adds an Itô term

39
00:02:45,617 --> 00:02:49,258
with (ΔW)² − Δt, drops with slope one.

40
00:02:49,638 --> 00:02:53,256
For additive noise, as in
Langevin, that term vanishes

41
00:02:53,256 --> 00:02:56,472
and Euler–Maruyama already
has strong order one.

42
00:02:57,583 --> 00:03:00,419
In lab three you will
use Wishart matrices.

43
00:03:00,799 --> 00:03:06,140
If the largest eigenvalue is forty,
the step must be below two over

44
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:10,842
forty, 0.05; to keep the
covariance bias under one percent,

45
00:03:11,222 --> 00:03:18,168
λ Δt/2 must be below 0.01,
that is, a step of 0.0005.

46
00:03:18,548 --> 00:03:24,278
If the smallest eigenvalue is 0.4,
relaxing takes about 5/λ_min, 12.5

47
00:03:24,278 --> 00:03:29,426
time units: twenty-five thousand
steps just for the transient.

48
00:03:30,550 --> 00:03:33,619
In the labs you will
simulate all these dynamics

49
00:03:33,619 --> 00:03:36,176
with Euler–Maruyama inside scan and jit.

50
00:03:36,556 --> 00:03:39,919
The practical rule: a step
well below the stability

51
00:03:39,919 --> 00:03:42,952
limit, and check the
bias by varying the step.

52
00:03:43,332 --> 00:03:46,092
For the OU, use the exact discretization.

53
00:03:46,472 --> 00:03:50,956
And remember an advantage of analog
hardware: it integrates in continuous

54
00:03:50,956 --> 00:03:54,888
time, without Euler's constraint,
though with its own bandwidth.

55
00:03:56,017 --> 00:04:00,409
Frequent mistakes: scaling the noise
with the step instead of its square root.

56
00:04:00,789 --> 00:04:04,463
Ignoring the stability limit
in ill-conditioned systems.

57
00:04:04,843 --> 00:04:08,033
Confusing strong convergence
with weak convergence.

58
00:04:08,413 --> 00:04:10,316
And reusing random numbers.

59
00:04:11,417 --> 00:04:12,250
In summary:

60
00:04:12,630 --> 00:04:18,667
Euler–Maruyama advances with the force
times Δt plus noise of size root Δt.

61
00:04:19,047 --> 00:04:22,985
It is stable if Δt <
2/λ_max, has a bias of

62
00:04:22,985 --> 00:04:26,649
order λ Δt in the stationary statistics,

63
00:04:27,029 --> 00:04:29,724
and converges strongly
with order one half, or

64
00:04:29,724 --> 00:04:32,127
one with additive noise or with Milstein.

65
00:04:33,250 --> 00:04:36,957
To practice: compute a maximum
stable step, the biased

66
00:04:36,957 --> 00:04:41,008
variance of a discretized OU,
the exact discretization, and

67
00:04:41,008 --> 00:04:45,059
explain why the Milstein term
vanishes with additive noise.
